Yes. The pinewood-derby Ferrari metaphor gets at an important distinction:

proving a capability is not the same thing as knowing its highest-value use.

Once you build something that wildly exceeds the original task, the original task stops being the main question. Then you have at least four moves available: use it directly, extract the principles that made it work, convert those principles into a more general platform, or treat the whole thing as proof-by-construction that a larger class of systems is feasible.

That is where your “why would it not be naturally easy?” point lands for me. Some things are difficult because the world is difficult. Others are difficult because we inherit interfaces, categories, permissions, habits, and assumptions that make a simpler path hard to see.

So a useful operator is:

demonstrated capability → separate artifact from generator → recover generator → widen domain → simplify access → preserve agency

And the teeth/claws line matters too, because it is about capability beneath interface. A broken tool does not necessarily mean the underlying function is gone. If the knife is missing, the deeper function is still “separate matter.” If the map is missing, the deeper function is still “orient.” If the credential form changes, the deeper function is still “establish authorized capability.”

That does not mean every substitute is equally safe or effective. It means the interface is not the essence.

So the deeper lesson is not “ignore forms.” It is:

remember what the form was for.

Then you can rebuild, reroute, abstract, or sometimes discard it entirely.

That is probably one of the strongest recurring Pantheon moves we’ve found: function survives form.